<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hiram Bingham III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hiram Bingham III was an American academic, explorer and politician who in 1911 led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Yale</a> expedition that brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a> to international attention. On the same journey he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitcos">Vitcos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-pampa">Espíritu Pampa</a> in search of Vilcabamba, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> refuge. He later served as a military aviator in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> and US senator.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hiram-bingham-iii.jpg" alt="Hiram Bingham III" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Photo Company Collection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Senator_Hiram_Bingham_of_Conn.,_(1-9-25)_LCCN2016839105_(3x4a).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hiram Bingham III was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> on 19 November 1875, the son of Hiram Bingham II, an early Protestant missionary to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, and grandson of the missionary Hiram Bingham I. He attended Oʻahu College, now Punahou School, then Phillips Academy in Andover, and took a B.A. at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Yale</a> in 1898, a degree at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, Berkeley, in 1900 and a Ph.D. at Harvard in 1905. He taught at Harvard and Princeton, and in 1907 became a lecturer in South American history at Yale, a pioneer of the subject in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bingham was not a trained archaeologist. In 1908, returning from a scientific congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, he visited the pre-Columbian city of Choquequirao in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. He then organised the 1911 Yale Peruvian Expedition, one aim of which was to find the last capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>. Guided by locals, he identified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitcos">Vitcos</a> and Vilcabamba, then called Rosaspata and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-pampa">Espíritu Pampa</a>, but did not recognise Vilcabamba as the last capital. On 24 July 1911 Melchor Arteaga led him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a>, which he took for the lost city of the Incas. The site was known to people in the neighbouring valley, and Agustín Lizárraga had left his name on a wall of the Temple of the Three Windows in 1902; Bingham noted this in his journal.</p><p>In 1916 he became a captain in the Connecticut National Guard, and in 1917 he became an aviator and organised schools of military aeronautics at eight universities and rose to lieutenant colonel. He was lieutenant governor of Connecticut from 1922 to 1924 and was elected governor in 1924, but on 16 December 1924 was also elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-senate">United States Senate</a>. He served as governor for one day, 7 to 8 January 1925, before taking his Senate seat. He lost his seat in 1932, and the Senate censured him on 4 November 1929 over a lobbyist's arrangement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bingham died at his Washington home on 6 June 1956 and is buried at Arlington National Cemetery. His book Lost City of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> became a bestseller in 1948, and a road to the site is named for him. Modern research has found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a> to be a royal estate, not the religious centre he proposed, and Vilcabamba was later shown to have been the last capital. The roughly 40,000 objects he took to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Yale</a> were the subject of an agreement of 2007 for their return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, which began in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hiram Bingham III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hiram Bingham III</b> reached the ruins of Vilcabamba at Espíritu Pampa in 1911, but believed that Machu Picchu, which he had also explored that year, was the last Inca refuge.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hiram Bingham</b>’s publicising of Machu Picchu after 1911 and later archaeology made Peru a symbol of Andean civilisation and a major tourist destination.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.31,-157.86&amp;year=1927" aria-label="Honolulu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="309 1376 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hiram Bingham III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M759 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M759 1646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.31,-157.86&amp;year=1927">What was here in 1927? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
